Qore dependency backports for Debian 13 and Ubuntu 26.04
======================================================

Copyright 2026 Qore Technologies, s.r.o.

The testing PPA is:

  https://launchpad.net/~qore-packagers/+archive/ubuntu/qore-testing
  dput destination: ppa:qore-packagers/qore-testing

The source preparer supports Ubuntu 26.04 (resolute, the default) and Debian 13
(trixie). Each target has its own changelog and version suffix. Debian sources
have no PPA destination: build and publish them in a separate Debian archive.
Build and qualify each distribution and architecture separately. These recipes
do not establish compatibility with Ubuntu 24.04 or Ubuntu 22.04.

Sources and package scope
-------------------------

sources.json pins upstream archives and existing Debian/Ubuntu packaging by
HTTPS URL and SHA-256 digest. It records the changelog date, target distribution,
maintainer, and target versions. The preparation tool additionally verifies the
c-ares, nghttp2, nghttp3, and ngtcp2 upstream signatures using keys shipped in
the pinned Debian packaging. The tree-sitter GitHub tag archive is pinned by
SHA-256; it does not have a release archive signature in this recipe.

  c-ares 1.34.8: Debian library packaging, plus both Qore DNS query fixes.
  nghttp2 1.70.0: Debian library, application, and documentation packages.
  nghttp3 1.18.0: Debian library packaging updated from 1.17.0.
  ngtcp2 1.25.0: Debian packaging updated from 1.24.0, including both OpenSSL
    and GnuTLS crypto backends.
  qore-tree-sitter 0.26.13: C runtime and development packages only, retaining
    Debian's SONAME-qualified library package and symbol history.

The trixie target additionally prepares qore-onnx 1.20.0 and ONNX Runtime
1.23.2+dfsg from Debian's repacked source archives, pinned by SHA-256 from
authenticated Debian source metadata. Ubuntu uses its archive's ONNX packages.
It also prepares Boost 1.90 and NATS C 3.13 for the independent process and
nats modules. These four recipes are Debian-only; selecting them for resolute
fails before preparing a source workspace.
ONNX Runtime derives its Python library links from VERSION_NUMBER instead of
a stale hard-coded version. The ABI-version generator writes SOVERSION_NUMBER
so an in-source build cannot overwrite the upstream release version.

Debian unstable's ONNX 1.20 library has a different protobuf ABI from Debian
13's ONNX 1.17 and replaces the libraries used by stable PyTorch. The qore-onnx
source instead builds libqore-onnx-dev: static PIC libraries in a private
multiarch prefix, headers in /usr/include/qore-onnx, and the qore_onnx C++
namespace. It supplies no system ONNX headers, shared library or Python module.
The exported CMake targets carry the private header path and protobuf link
dependencies. Both libraries use protobuf-lite; ONNX Runtime binds its legacy
namespace references to the configured private namespace at its internal
protobuf boundary. Static schema registration is disabled because the runtime
registers its own schemas; standalone consumers and the C++ tests initialize
their schemas explicitly. ONNX Runtime links this private dependency; Debian's
ONNX/PyTorch packages remain installed and usable. Consumers must be rebuilt when this
static dependency receives security updates. The incorporating binary packages
record its exact source version in Static-Built-Using and ship its copyright
and license record as copyright.qore-onnx. Keep the matching qore-onnx source
available alongside the runtime packages.

Debian's ONNX Runtime repack excludes models whose licenses are unclear and
disables the upstream model-dependent unit tests. Its installed-package
tests cover C linking, generated-model inference and CPU execution providers.
An additional test imports Debian's ONNX/PyTorch and the backported runtime in
both orders, compares convolution results, and checks profiling records for
actual CPU, DNNL and XNNPACK execution. The runtime also backports the upstream
XNNPACK Gemm dynamic-batch fix. Unsupported transpose/bias layouts fall back to
the CPU provider, and runtime input channels are checked against the weights.
Numerical regressions cover changing and empty batches, optional and broadcast
biases, transposed inputs/weights, zero input channels and mismatched shapes.
qore-onnx runs the upstream C++ tests during its build and has an installed
CMake/linking test alongside Debian's ONNX and PyTorch packages.
These tests must pass for both architectures; a successful build alone is
not qualification.

ONNX Runtime keeps compressed debug information in each library's debug file.
This avoids dwz 0.15 generating an empty, string-only shared debug object
(https://bugs.debian.org/1106590); automatic debug-symbol packages remain enabled.

qore-tree-sitter is deliberately a separate source package. It produces
libtree-sitter0.26 and libtree-sitter-dev, without backporting Rust crates or the
tree-sitter CLI. Qore builds its committed generated parser directly. It does
not need the generator installed to build astparser. The C runtime has an
offline C API/linking smoke test and an installed-package test; this does not
replace upstream's full Rust/parser regression suite.

Independent module dependencies
-------------------------------

The Boost backport keeps Debian's complete versioned library/development
package recipe and installed-package tests. Its Boost.Build engine uses the
supported cxx bootstrap toolset with Debian's compiler and linker flags. The
default gcc bootstrap strips b2 early and drops linker hardening; the cxx
toolset instead leaves stripping and detached debug symbols to debhelper.
Bootstrap failures must stop the build. The redundant unversioned g++ build
dependency is removed because build-essential already supplies it.
The CMake configuration generator uses paths relative to the installed config
instead of embedding Debian's temporary staging directory. Preprocessed
assembly receives Debian's compiler flags and an assembler debug-prefix map,
so Boost.Context debug information does not retain the build directory.

Boost 1.90 development headers replace the distribution's 1.83 development
headers, while the versioned 1.83 runtime packages remain coinstallable.
Qualification must exercise existing 1.83 clients after that transition and
load both runtime versions in one process in both orders. The process module
still carries its own patched Boost.Process headers; this backport supplies
the matching Boost headers and libraries and does not replace that patch.

NATS C retains Debian's library/development packages. The recipe adds a
bounded C API probe against a private loopback nats-server with JetStream,
covering pub/sub, request/reply, missing keys and key/value revision history.
The probe runs against the newly built library and again against the installed
SDK. It needs no external service or network access. Invoke the broker by its
installed /usr/sbin/nats-server path because unprivileged test users may not
have /usr/sbin in PATH. Qualification also checks that existing 3.10 clients
keep using their original runtime after the 3.13 SDK is installed.

Both backports require native amd64 and arm64 builds, source/binary Lintian,
reproducibility, ELF hardening, matching detached debug symbols and installed
tests before accepting the downstream modules. Keep the source and dependency
maintenance records with the binary artifacts; these recipes do not establish
a published Debian archive or a final Qore release.

c-ares patch maintenance
------------------------

The source preparer generates debian/patches/qore-lost-query.patch using
cmake/PatchCaresLostQuery.cmake from this Qore checkout. This is the same
patcher used by the ordinary FetchContent build. Both changes are required:

  * A second detach must not remove a different query which reused its ID.
  * The send path must compare the query identity, rather than just checking
    whether that query ID is present, to avoid accessing a freed query.

The generated patch is added to Debian's quilt series and included in the
source upload. It is applied by dpkg-source, without CMake downloading or
patching libraries during the Qore package build. A changed upstream source
that no longer matches the patcher fails preparation and requires review.

The trixie recipe also moves c-ares' timeout-selection test to its existing
IPv4/IPv6 loopback DNS mock. The original test tries the host's external DNS
server; an immediate unreachable-network error leaves no timer to inspect.
The corrected test retains the timer assertions and checks successful query
completion without external network access. Both runtime fixes are unchanged.

When updating c-ares, check whether upstream includes BOTH fixes before
removing the patch. Also increment the PPA revision when modifying a published
patch. Keep the system-library backport and FetchContent fixes synchronized.

Preparing unsigned source uploads
--------------------------------

Use the distribution's /usr/bin/python3 (3.11 or newer, with tarfile's data
extraction filter), dpkg-dev, cmake, gpg and gpgv. On Ubuntu, install these with
APT. Generated archives and extracted sources must live outside the checkout:

  tools/prepare-debian-backports.py \
    --output /path/to/new-source-workspace \
    --cache /path/to/download-cache

For Debian 13, add --suite trixie. The same pinned upstream sources and patches
are used, with ~deb13.1 versions instead of ~ubuntu26.04.1. Changelog entries and
source provenance refer to the selected distribution. --suite resolute selects
the existing Ubuntu target explicitly.

Pass source package names after the options to prepare a subset. The tool
refuses existing package workspaces. After populating the download cache,
--offline repeats preparation without network access in another new workspace.

Only the pristine SOURCE preparation uses dpkg-buildpackage -d -nc; native
Build-Depends need not be installed to assemble an upload. Binary builds must
resolve Build-Depends and run their tests. The source uploads include the orig
tarballs (-sa), upstream signatures where available, .dsc, .debian.tar.xz,
source .buildinfo and source .changes. They are unsigned.

The source versions sort above the required upstream version and below the
corresponding future official Debian revision. Do not reuse a version after
Launchpad has accepted it; increment its Qore revision in sources.json.

Clean local binary builds
-------------------------

The helper uses rootless Podman with an Ubuntu 26.04 image by default. It
installs tools and declared build dependencies in a fresh container, then builds
and tests as an unprivileged build user from the .dsc with the network disabled. It does
not install backports into the development host or change the host's APT sources.

  podman pull docker.io/library/ubuntu:26.04
  tools/build-debian-backport.sh \
    /path/to/new-source-workspace/c-ares /path/to/new-c-ares-results

For a prepared Debian 13 source package, select the Debian image:

  podman pull docker.io/library/debian:13
  QORE_BACKPORT_IMAGE=docker.io/library/debian:13 \
    tools/build-debian-backport.sh \
      /path/to/trixie-sources/c-ares /path/to/new-trixie-c-ares-results

The helper requires the source changelog suite to match the container release.
Run it on a native amd64 or arm64 host; an Ubuntu-built binary is not a Debian
build. These five recipes supply the HTTP/DNS/parser libraries. Debian 13 also
needs the ONNX/ONNX Runtime recipes and Arrow/Parquet packages before Qore can
build with all required features; see debian/README.source.

Build qore-onnx first. To build ONNX Runtime against its local dependency,
set QORE_BACKPORT_DEPENDENCIES to the same-architecture qore-onnx results:

  QORE_BACKPORT_IMAGE=docker.io/library/debian:13 \
  QORE_BACKPORT_DEPENDENCIES=/path/to/trixie-qore-onnx-results \
    tools/build-debian-backport.sh \
      /path/to/trixie-sources/onnxruntime /path/to/new-trixie-onnxruntime-results

The directory is mounted read-only. Its .deb files are installed only inside
the provisioning container, and their SHA-256 hashes are retained with the
build results. The subsequent build and tests still have no network access.

Repeat for nghttp2, nghttp3, ngtcp2 and qore-tree-sitter. Each results directory
must be new. Results include binaries, build metadata, logs, the base image's
identity and the exact build-dependency inventory. The provisioned build image
is retained locally for diagnosis; its ID is recorded in build-image.txt.
QORE_BACKPORT_IMAGE may select a previously pulled supported base image by
digest. No image is pushed to a registry.

Lintian errors in source or binary packages fail the build helper. Review
warnings as well: inherited nghttp2 packaging includes a metapackage with no
payload and Sphinx JavaScript
copies in its documentation. Ubuntu 26.04's Lintian also reports the newer
Standards-Version and backport revision conventions in some source packages.
Passing this helper is not a claim of full
autopkgtest, installation/upgrade, multi-architecture, or reproducibility
qualification. The equivalent official builder is sbuild:

  sbuild --chroot-mode=schroot -d resolute --arch=amd64 -A /path/to/package.dsc

Before uploading, verify the resulting source package with:

  lintian --fail-on error /path/to/package_source.changes

Signing and uploading
---------------------

The following PPA upload instructions apply only to resolute sources. Do not
upload trixie packages to the Ubuntu PPA; Debian publication requires its own
signed archive and distribution-specific qualification.

Source preparation and local builds do not contact Launchpad to publish
anything. Review the source changes, build results and PPA target first.
Transfer each source upload's complete set of files to the host holding the
registered signing key. Keep the private key on that host. For each reviewed
source .changes file, on that host:

  debsign -k FULL_PRIMARY_FINGERPRINT /path/to/package_source.changes
  dput ppa:qore-packagers/qore-testing /path/to/package_source.changes

Use the SOURCE .changes files from the preparation workspace, not the binary
.changes files from the build results. Each uploader signs with their own
registered key and must have upload rights to the team's PPA. Check the PPA's
enabled architectures against the architectures actually qualified locally.
Local validation initially uses native amd64.

Wait for all dependency binaries to build and publish for resolute before
uploading Qore. Qore's Build-Depends can then be resolved from the same PPA.
YAML and its companion user modules are bundled in qore-stdlib; debugger tools
do not need a separate qore-yaml-module package.

